\alias{LearnerRegrLimiX}
\title{LimiX Regression Learner}
\description{
Tabular foundation model developed by Stable AI.
Uses \CRANpkg{reticulate} to interface with the \href{https://github.com/limix-ldm-ai/LimiX}{\code{LimiX}} Python package.
Calls \code{LimiXPredictor()} from the Python module \code{limix}.
The model makes predictions via in-context learning and is not trained on the data in the usual sense.
\verb{$train()} therefore only stores the training data and loads the pre-trained weights,
and the whole computation happens in \verb{$predict()}.
Features are passed to \code{LimiX} as a data frame, so that categorical features are ordinal encoded and missing
values are handled by the package's own preprocessing pipeline.
}
\section{Installation}{

This learner relies on \CRANpkg{reticulate} to handle Python dependencies.
It is not necessary to install any Python package manually in advance or specify a Python environment
via \code{reticulate::use_python()}, \code{reticulate::use_virtualenv()}, \code{reticulate::use_condaenv()},
or \code{reticulate::use_miniconda()}.
By calling \verb{$train()} or \verb{$predict()}, the required Python packages will be installed automatically,
if not already.
\code{LimiX} is not released on PyPI and is installed from its
\href{https://github.com/limix-ldm-ai/LimiX}{GitHub repository}, which requires \code{git} and Python 3.12 or newer.
Reticulate will then configure and initialize an ephemeral environment satisfying those requirements,
unless an existing environment (e.g., \code{"r-reticulate"}) in reticulate's
\href{https://rstudio.github.io/reticulate/articles/versions.html#order-of-discovery}{Order of Discovery}
contains all the necessary packages.

You may also manually install \code{LimiX} into a Python environment following the
\href{https://github.com/limix-ldm-ai/LimiX#-tutorials}{official installation guide}
and specify the environment via \verb{reticulate::use_*()} before calling \verb{$train()} or \verb{$predict()}.
Besides \code{LimiX}, the environment needs the Python packages \code{huggingface_hub} and \code{triton}.
\code{LimiX} imports \code{triton}, which is only available on Linux, so the learner is only tested on Linux.

On the first call to \verb{$train()}, the pre-trained model weights are downloaded from
\href{https://huggingface.co/stable-ai}{Hugging Face} and cached for subsequent calls
(about 1.6 GB for \code{"LimiX-2"} and 10 MB for \code{"LimiX-2M"}).
The weights have their own licenses, and the weights of \code{"LimiX-2"} are released under a non-commercial license.
The \code{"LimiX-2"} model is slow without a GPU.
}

\section{Saving a Learner}{

In order to save a \code{LearnerRegrLimiX} for later usage,
it is necessary to call the \verb{$marshal()} method on the \code{Learner}
before writing it to disk, as the object will otherwise not be saved correctly.
After loading a marshaled \code{LearnerRegrLimiX} into R again,
you then need to call \verb{$unmarshal()} to transform it into a useable state.
The pre-trained weights are not serialized;
they are dropped on \verb{$marshal()} and reloaded from the Hugging Face cache or \code{model_path} on \verb{$unmarshal()}.
}

